        "type": "example",
        "title": "Kurosawa's Throne of Blood: Macbeth Without Shakespeare",
        "content": "The boldest experiment of the three is **Akira Kurosawa's *Throne of Blood* (Japanese title *Kumonosu-jō*, 'Spider's Web Castle,' 1957)**. It contains **not a single line of Shakespeare**. Kurosawa relocates the story to feudal Japan, renames Macbeth **Washizu** (played by his great collaborator **Toshiro Mifune**), and retells the tragedy through the visual grammar of **Noh theatre** — Japan's centuries-old, highly stylized drama of masks, slow movement, and ghostly restraint. Critics, including the great T. S. Eliot admirer and film scholar tradition, have often called it the finest *Macbeth* on film precisely *because* it abandons the words and keeps the spirit.\n\nSeveral choices become brilliant acts of interpretation. The three witches become a **single spirit**, an old spinner at a wheel in the fog of Spider's Web Forest, humming about the vanity of human ambition — the spinning wheel making fate literal. Lady Macbeth (Asaji) barely moves; her face is a Noh mask of stillness, and her famous hand-washing becomes a silent, mesmerizing gesture that says more than any soliloquy. Kurosawa externalizes the play's images: where Shakespeare writes 'Birnam wood,' Kurosawa gives us a real forest that seems to *rise up and advance* on the castle in a genuinely terrifying sequence.\n\nAnd the ending is the most famous change in all Macbeth adaptation. Shakespeare has Macduff behead Macbeth offstage. Kurosawa has Washizu's **own soldiers turn on him** and kill him with a storm of arrows — dozens of them thudding into the walls around him, then into his body, one finally piercing his neck. Mifune's terror is real; Kurosawa reportedly used live archers. It is a staggering image of a tyrant destroyed by the very men he ruled, and it visualizes the theme — that a usurper forfeits all loyalty — more powerfully than any speech. *Throne of Blood* proves the deepest truth about adaptation: fidelity is not about words. You can throw away every line and be more faithful to *Macbeth* than a word-perfect production that misses its soul.",
